            <ArticleTitle>&quot;Dual role&quot; guiding catheter: a new technique for patients requiring coronary protection during transcatheter aortic valve implantation.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>131-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s12928-015-0326-4</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Coronary occlusion is a life-threatening complication during transcatheter aortic valve implantation (TAVI) that requires immediate revascularization. Conventionally, a total of 3 catheters are required to protect the coronary ostia during TAVI: a pigtail catheter for the angiographic evaluation of valve positioning, a guiding catheter, and a TAVI delivery system. In this case series, we present a new, simplified technique for patients who require coronary protection during TAVI using a total of only 2 catheters: a guiding catheter and TAVI delivery system. This simplified technique results in a simpler procedure and reduces vascular complications in this high-risk cohort.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
